WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.080
So, it took me 40 years to get to the

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:04.920
point where I built a company that

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:06.680
actually runs before I step foot in the

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.080
door. Everything I've learned has gotten

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:11.600
me here, and not only taken time, but

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:13.240
man, millions of dollars lost, dozens of

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.400
mistakes. So, I'm going to give you

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:17.360
every piece of business advice I learned

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:20.800
along the way, the hard way, and that I

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.800
wish I knew years earlier.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.080
So, I hate to say it, but here's some

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:29.240
truths. The first truth is the people

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:31.040
that love you, they don't actually want

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:33.000
what's best for you. And when I realized

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.400
this, it hit me like a brick wall. Like,

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.280
think about this. Your friends, your

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:39.040
family, your partner, they do love you

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:40.600
endlessly, but love isn't always fair.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.720
Like, family wants you to be safe and

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:44.480
secure. The risk that you're taking is

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:46.960
probably terrifying to them. And the

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:49.640
problem is that risk is exactly where

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.280
all the money lives. I spent 10 years

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:53.560
hopping between five different finance

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:56.600
firms, and every time someone in my

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:59.920
family said, "Why?" They were supportive

00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:01.800
on the surface, but every jump came with

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:03.800
the same questions. Is the grass really

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.239
greener? Are you sure this next thing is

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:07.600
actually going to make you happy? And in

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:10.160
some cases they were right, but I think

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.600
long-term trajectory, meta, they

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.840
weren't. Basically, if I had listened to

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.240
them, my life would have been completely

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.000
different, and I would have not been as

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.240
successful. And the wild part is in the

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.240
grand scheme of things, taking a career

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:23.240
risk literally was almost no risk. Every

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:25.160
time I made more money, every time I

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:27.120
grew and learned something different,

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:28.040
but I listened to them for so long

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.600
because they thought, "Oh, you've

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:31.600
already reached the pinnacle. Why keep

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.360
climbing?" And I think one of the best

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.600
pieces of advice I ever got on this

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.160
actually came from a monk, which is wild

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:37.800
to say.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:40.840
>> In the first few months of going to the

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:42.320
monastery, I felt very um selfish that,

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:44.520
you know, I have a loving family, and I

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:46.320
left them, especially my mom, who I love

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.760
very much, and and I went up to my guru,

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.440
and I said, you know, "Am I selfish to

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.040
be living here by myself?" And he

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.200
reached over to his box of Kleenex, and

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.120
he pulled out a tissue and laid it on

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.560
the table, and he said, "You're in the

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.600
middle, and your mom's in this corner,

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.440
your dad's in this corner, and in

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.960
brother's hand, other corner." And he

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:01.240
grabbed the middle and he lifted it up

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.400
and he says, "As you uplift yourself,

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:05.680
you lift uplift everyone that you're

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:07.160
energetically connected to." In essence,

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:09.399
what he was saying is that the work that

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:11.240
you're doing on yourself,

00:02:09.399 --> 00:02:14.320
though you might think you're doing it

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:16.080
in isolation, you're actually impacting

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:17.640
so many people around you.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.720
>> So, I mean, if you think about it that

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:22.520
way, separation isn't betrayal. It's

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:24.680
literally just physics. Like, you start

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:26.480
working more, drinking it less, having

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:29.640
conversations nobody in your old circle

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:31.200
can follow like yet. And they love you,

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:32.360
but they actually pull you back down

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.920
because that's where they are and they

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:35.720
want to be close to you. It's not always

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.680
cuz they want you to fail. It's because

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:39.680
your growth is proof their world is

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.840
about to change, too. And that terrifies

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:42.600
people. And maybe you're going to leave

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:44.520
them behind. And they don't like that.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:46.920
And so, I I realized a line that's

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:49.120
really important. Risk always looks

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:51.520
reckless to the people standing still.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:53.800
It only stops looking crazy once you can

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.480
start lifting them with you. But by

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:57.480
then, you don't need their permission.

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:59.160
You've already got the results. So, I

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.040
think you got to ask yourself, what's

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:02.840
your goal, really? Is it comfort? Is it

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:04.760
something bigger? Are you really being

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:06.080
supported? Are you wearing a life vest?

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:08.040
The older you get, the clearer this

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:09.600
becomes. If you sit in the same chair

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.040
and talk to the same five people every

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.400
single day, nothing new is ever going to

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.480
happen. Like, opportunity, it turns out,

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:17.519
I've realized, does not knock. You have

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:19.680
to go chase it. And it's why I've come

00:03:17.519 --> 00:03:21.360
to this conclusion, which is working

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.760
from home is the worst thing that can

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:25.600
ever happen to you. And if you want to

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:28.440
make more money, get promoted, learn

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:30.120
faster, level up, you have to be around

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:31.519
winners in person if you want to win.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:33.560
And the hard part is they're like not on

00:03:31.519 --> 00:03:36.480
your couch. You have to leave your room.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:38.560
Because being poor is contagious, and so

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:40.320
is being rich. And I know that's not the

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:42.720
popular answer right now, and people

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:44.239
hate me for it sometimes, but it's true.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.400
The internet, for some reason, treats

00:03:44.239 --> 00:03:48.480
work from home as the best thing ever. I

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:51.880
think that's ridiculous. Remote work

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:54.600
optimizes for your comfort. Growth is

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:57.000
not about comfort. And you don't get

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:58.600
that friction, that difficulty from

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.920
sitting on your couch. And so like when

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:02.240
we built out this office, people started

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:04.080
choosing to come in on the weekends.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:06.040
That wasn't because anyone made them,

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:07.959
but because they actually wanted to be

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:09.560
better every single day. Some of these

00:04:07.959 --> 00:04:11.560
people work until, you know, 12:00 in

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:12.959
the morning sometimes. And then maybe

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:14.520
they take off a couple weeks. I don't

00:04:12.959 --> 00:04:16.040
think you can build that kind of pull

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:18.600
through a laptop screen. And if you're

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:21.160
remote because it's genuinely the only

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:22.200
way the job can exist, okay, fine. But

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:23.880
if you're remote because it's more

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:25.280
comfortable, I'm sorry you're trading

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:27.000
growth for convenience and I wish young

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:29.120
Cody would have known this cuz I would

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:31.080
have made so much more money faster. I

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.840
worked remote for a number of years and

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:34.640
I watched other people get along farther

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:36.840
than me and I wondered why cuz I was a

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:38.320
really high performer and that was why.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:39.919
The real players are actually out there

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:41.080
meeting face-to-face and you just like

00:04:39.919 --> 00:04:42.120
people more and you give them more

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:43.480
opportunities when you meet

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:45.200
face-to-face. It's really not fair, but

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:46.800
it is the way it is. And so I think one

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:49.320
of the biggest changes you can start

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:51.240
making right away is making your way

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:53.560
into rooms where the high net worth, the

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:55.360
hard workers live. It is true that

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:56.720
you're you're like basically exactly as

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:58.600
successful as the people you surround

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:00.320
yourself with. If somebody's stuck at

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:02.360
the same level as you,

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:03.400
okay, you can still be around them, but

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:05.480
you got to find people who've already

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:07.640
broken through. It turns out proximity

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:09.680
is not a nice-to-have. It's a must-have.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:11.040
And it took me 25 years of advice to

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:12.480
figure that out. One of the other

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:15.000
lessons I wish I learned earlier was

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:17.560
nobody's going to advocate for you more

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:19.360
than you. So early on in my career,

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:21.360
I decided to do something unhinged. I

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:23.760
flew myself to Goldman Sachs

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:25.360
headquarters in person for a role. Most

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:26.760
people, you know, you apply online and

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:28.720
never hear back. I stayed there for 2

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:30.400
days. I did multiple interviews, a full

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:32.880
PowerPoint presentation I prepared and

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:34.800
delivered live. I had people beat up my

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:36.200
PowerPoint live, which was awful, you

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:37.560
know, telling me flat out, "I don't

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:38.720
believe you. What's this? What do you

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:41.000
know about that?" I ended up getting the

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:43.000
job because I advocated for myself, I

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:44.440
got in person and I showed up. And then

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:47.000
when I wanted to hit the big leagues and

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:49.040
go to Credit Suisse, one of the biggest

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:50.920
investment firms in the world. I flew

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:52.800
myself on my own dime to three

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:55.160
continents. I flew myself to London,

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:56.960
their headquarters in New York, and I

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:58.400
flew myself to San Francisco where one

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.960
of the partners was in. So, I didn't

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:02.800
even end up getting that job, but that

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:05.280
same guy, Ryan uh Schlitt, shout out

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:06.960
Ryan, he helped me eventually raise

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:09.520
millions of dollars for something else

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:11.400
that I did. He eventually circled back

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:13.080
and offered me that role at a different

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:14.760
date. And even when they slammed the

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:17.160
door in my face, I just kept my little

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:18.720
toesie just slightly in there so that

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:21.400
eventually I could get back in. The

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:23.760
world will not hand you access. You have

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:25.919
to go out and get it yourself loudly,

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:27.320
uninvited if you have to. The second

00:06:25.919 --> 00:06:29.000
half is what you do once you're actually

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:31.640
in the room. So, I came out of college

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:33.880
making 37K a year. By the time I left

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:35.520
finance, I was making seven figures as a

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:37.520
partner, and that was working for

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:39.400
somebody else. I was still an employee.

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:41.800
I didn't get there asking for 3% raises

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:43.320
every year. I got there by investing in

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:45.560
what I was doing. I got there by

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:47.760
negotiating my worth based on how much

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:49.640
growth or revenue I drove the company.

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:51.440
Salary was only about 20% of what I

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:54.320
actually made. The rest came from me

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:56.800
betting on myself, doing bonuses, asking

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:59.240
for commissions on deals. All of it tied

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:01.720
to like results instead of just me

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:03.760
showing up. Dude, hard piece of advice,

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:05.600
nobody cares about how hard you work.

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:07.760
They only care about what your outcomes

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:09.360
are. If you can't deliver,

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:11.160
it doesn't matter if you killed yourself

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:12.360
to do it. And I wish somebody had told

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:13.919
me that earlier because I really

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:16.240
associated a lot with being a hard

00:07:13.919 --> 00:07:18.720
worker. And it turns out also, you know,

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:21.080
most people, small business owners or

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:23.200
regular employees, you don't negotiate

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:24.919
anything. You know, you pay your vendors

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:27.320
whatever number gets sent on the

00:07:24.919 --> 00:07:28.840
invoice. They accept the first offer on

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:30.880
the deal. You just won't get what you

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:32.880
don't ask for. No one is going to hand

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:34.600
you anything. You can never assume

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:36.520
you're going to get anything. And every

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:37.600
time you don't ask for something in the

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:40.320
right way, you're leaving money in

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:42.440
somebody else's pocket. Make sure

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:44.720
that you're filling yours first. The

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:46.160
next thing I think so few people do it's

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:48.760
almost painful. Don't be afraid to

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:50.960
promote. If your work isn't seen, it

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:53.000
doesn't exist. Be a promoter. People

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:55.000
talk about that like it's a bad thing.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:57.000
You know, when I walked into Goldman as

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:58.480
a public school kid, I was in a room

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:00.880
full of people who'd been preparing for

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:02.960
that opportunity their entire adult

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:05.080
lives. They went to the Ivy Leagues.

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:06.440
They knew the vocabulary. They summered

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:08.520
in places. Like I didn't even know

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:10.480
summering was a verb. I couldn't out

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:12.000
credential them. And I couldn't out

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:13.640
network them. Like I didn't even know

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:15.760
any rich people. The only lever I had

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:17.280
left was duration. I was like, well, I'm

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:18.960
going to outlast every [ __ ] in

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:21.160
this room, you know? So, I was the first

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:22.880
one in every morning and the last one

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:25.120
out every night. And I know that sounds

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:26.640
old school and lame, but it wasn't that

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:29.480
much earlier. Like a lot of people came

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:31.040
in at 7:30. I was in at 6:30. And there

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:33.400
was a managing director in our group

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:34.800
named Jim, former military, hardcore. I

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:37.599
don't think he talked to any of us cuz

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:39.479
we were little peon juniors. He came in

00:08:37.599 --> 00:08:40.840
at 6:30 every morning. And I just kept

00:08:39.479 --> 00:08:42.360
coming in a little bit earlier every day

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:44.280
until I figured out what time he came in

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:45.920
at. And then I made sure I was there

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:48.040
before him. And I made sure I was still

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:50.080
there when he left. I didn't wait for

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:51.680
someone to notice my work on its own. I

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:54.760
engineered the moment where it couldn't

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:57.360
be missed. And eventually my performance

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:58.640
plus my hard work made him notice it.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:00.080
And once he did, he started handing me

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:01.760
opportunities nobody else on the team

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:03.600
got. I think, [music] you know, what I

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:05.560
wish I realized earlier is like that's

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:07.880
not my manager's job or your manager's

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:09.400
job to know your work. [music]

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:10.920
Nobody should be auditing your effort

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:13.200
behind the scenes, tallying up the

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:15.360
hours, waiting to reward you. Your

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:16.839
manager's busy, right? They're their own

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:18.520
boss. They got their own fires to put

00:09:16.839 --> 00:09:21.040
out. If your best work is happening

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:23.000
quietly where no one can see it, it's

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:24.839
functionally identical to the work that

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:26.600
never happened at all. And this goes for

00:09:24.839 --> 00:09:28.200
you as the owner as well. Don't wait to

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:30.720
